#
# What turns the inject Envoy on (ADR-0040): the three values that differ from env.example, as an env
# file that goes IN FRONT of it (image/gateway/render takes the first definition of a key, so a
# file listed earlier wins). A deployment that enables envoy-inject.service writes these three lines
# into /etc/mediatore-gateway/env, above the rest, or lists this file first. Whole-line comments only;
# no '|' in a value. Nothing here is a secret.
#
# The data plane asks the authorizer's CHECK socket, a unix socket only the data plane's group can
# open and which answers yes or no, never a credential (ADR-0040 decision 6): with a credential in
# play, loopback TCP would let any local process ask the authorizer a question.
AUTHORIZER_ENDPOINT=pipe: { path: /run/mediatore-gateway/check/check.sock }
# With the check on a unix socket the data plane's user has no business on loopback at all: the
# host table's one loopback exception (ADR-0053 decision 5) is emptied.
CHECK_TCP_ELEMENTS=
# The host table admits the inject port to the sandbox range under the relay's per-source cap
# (ADR-0040 decision 7): the element is ", <INJECT_PORT>", appended to the relay's ports.
INJECT_PORT_ELEMENT=, 3133
